"comparison": "Goldman Sachs and JPMorgan Chase both describe a highly competitive environment where price is a significant basis for competition across their diverse businesses. GS notes that price competition has compressed bid/offer spreads affecting market-making, and generally faces pricing pressure (GS: \"Price competition has also led to compression in the difference between the price at which a market participant is willing to sell an instrument and the price at which another market participant is willing to buy it... which has affected our market-making businesses.\" and \"The increasing prevalence of passive investment strategies... has affected the competitive and pricing dynamics for our asset management products and services.\"). JPM explicitly states it competes on price and varies by product and geography (JPM: \"The Firm's businesses generally compete on the basis of the quality and variety of the Firm's products and services, transaction execution, innovation, reputation and price.\" and \"Competition also varies based on the types of clients, customers, industries and geographies served.\"). Both firms identify price/fees as a core competitive driver, though GS highlights the structural shift from passive investing while JPM lists price as one of several bases including innovation and quality.",
